          "ruleId": "CWE-1333",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Attacker-controlled regex pattern reaches new RegExp() and is executed against run text with no validation, enabling ReDoS (Regular Expression Denial of Service). Chain: POST /v1/checklists (packages/backend/src/api/v1/checklists.ts, checklists.post('/')) stores `data: FilterLogic` from ctx.request.body verbatim into the `checklist.data` column with zero schema validation (comment literally says 'TODO: full zod'). A checklist entry can be `{id:'regex', params:{regex:'<attacker pattern>', field:'output', type:'contains'}}`. Later, POST /v1/evaluations/run (packages/backend/src/api/v1/evaluations/index.ts, evaluations.post('/run')) reads that checklist's `data` as `checks`, builds `virtualRun` directly from the SAME request body's `input`/`output` fields, and calls `runChecksOnRun(virtualRun, checks)` (packages/backend/src/api/v1/evaluations/utils.ts:81, confirmed reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1). Because the 'regex' check has an `evaluator` (not `.sql`), `hasNonSQLFilter` routes it through `checkRun` -> `runner.evaluator!(run, params)` (packages/backend/src/checks/runChecks.ts:98) which dispatches to the CHECK_RUNNERS 'regex' entry in packages/backend/src/checks/index.ts: `const re = new RegExp(regex); const has = re.test(lastMsg(run[field]))`. Both the evil regex AND the text it's matched against (`output`/`input`) are fully attacker-controlled in a single request, so a catastrophic-backtracking pattern (e.g. `(a+)+$`) with a crafted non-matching string will hang the Node.js event loop. A second, independently-confirmed trigger path exists via POST /v1/radars (packages/backend/src/api/v1/radars.ts, radars.post('/')) which stores `checks: any[]` equally unvalidated into `radar.checks`; the background job packages/backend/src/jobs/radar.ts (`runRadarChecksOnRun`) later calls `runChecksOnRun(run, checks, true)` (line 12, reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1) which reaches the exact same `new RegExp(regex)` call (uid 3659ddc82e888cddad2a6d02f4f1412c3809d923, reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1) against real logged run data.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Independently re-derived the full source\u2192sink chain from the real source tree (not from the analyst's say-so):\n(1) packages/backend/src/api/v1/checklists.ts:33-52 `checklists.post(\"/\")` destructures `data` straight from `ctx.request.body` with only a type assertion (no zod/validation anywhere in the file's POST route) and inserts it verbatim as `checklist.data`.\n(2) packages/backend/src/api/v1/evaluations/index.ts:102-159 `evaluations.post(\"/run\")` reads `checklist`/`input`/`output` from the SAME request body, fetches the stored checklist by slug, sets `const checks = checklistData.data` (line 122), builds `virtualRun` whose `input`/`output` fields are the raw request body values (lines 124-146), and calls `runChecksOnRun(virtualRun, checks)` at line 153 \u2014 with zero sanitization of either the regex pattern or the text it's tested against.\n(3) packages/backend/src/checks/runChecks.ts: `hasNonSQLFilter` (lines 12-27) flags any check whose CHECK_RUNNERS entry has an `evaluator`; `checkRun` (lines 83-98) dispatches to `runner.evaluator!(run, params)` at line 98 for such checks.\n(4) packages/backend/src/checks/index.ts lines 95-113, the `regex` CHECK_RUNNERS entry: `const re = new RegExp(regex)` (line 100) then `re.test(lastMsg(run[field]))` (line 102) \u2014 both the pattern (`params.regex`) and the subject text (`run[field]`, i.e. attacker-supplied `output`/`input`) are fully attacker controlled within one authenticated request. A catastrophic-backtracking pattern (e.g. `(a+)+$`) against a crafted non-matching string will block Node's single-threaded event loop for the whole process (classic ReDoS, CWE-1333), impacting all tenants sharing that backend instance, not just the caller's own project.\nAlso verified the second independent trigger: packages/backend/src/api/v1/radars.ts:217-241 `radars.post(\"/\")` stores `checks: any[]` from the request body unvalidated into `radar.checks`; packages/backend/src/jobs/radar.ts:9-23 `runRadarChecksOnRun` reads `radar.checks` and calls `runChecksOnRun(run, checks, true)` at line 12, reaching the identical `new RegExp(regex)` sink against real logged run data from a background job (no request-time rate limit).\nGraph queries against scan_id 39391b6ee394d4f8ba76da9837645885da014f16 corroborate the sink's reachability: CpgCall uid 3659ddc82e888cddad2a6d02f4f1412c3809d923 (`new RegExp(regex)`, checks/index.ts:100) has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1, matching the analyst's citation exactly; the `runner.evaluator!(run, params)` call (runChecks.ts:98) is also reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1. The backend Koa `.post()` route-handler methods themselves (checklists.ts/evaluations/index.ts/radars.ts) show reachable_from_entry=false in the graph, but this is the documented static-CPG gap for dynamically-registered Koa router callbacks (router.post(path, handler) isn't modeled as a static call edge) \u2014 confirmed these ARE real, mounted HTTP routes by reading packages/backend/src/api/v1/index.ts (radars/evaluations/checklists routers all mounted via `v1.use(...)`), so the graph's reachable_from_entry=false on the route lambdas does not undermine the finding; the sink itself being marked reachable_from_entry=true/hop_distance=1 (from runChecksOnRun's internal call graph) is the stronger and corroborating signal. No zod/schema validation exists on any of the three POST bodies (grep for zod/validate/schema across packages/backend/src/api/v1 found none applied to checklists.ts POST, evaluations/index.ts, or radars.ts)."
          },
