          "ruleId": "CWE-942",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Koa CORS middleware (patchedCors / @koa/cors config) reflects the request's Origin header back as Access-Control-Allow-Origin ('ctx.get(\"Origin\") || \"*\"') while also setting Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true. The expected control here -- a fixed allow-list of trusted origins -- is absent; any third-party site can issue credentialed cross-origin requests (with the caller's bearer token/cookies) and read the response, defeating the same-origin policy for the whole API.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against source and graph. packages/backend/src/utils/cors.ts defines patchedCors, which for OPTIONS requests sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: ctx.get(\"Origin\") || \"*\"` and `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` (lines 7,9), and for all other methods delegates to @koa/cors with `origin(ctx){ return ctx.get(\"Origin\") || \"*\" }, credentials: true` (lines 17-24) \u2014 i.e. it reflects whatever Origin the caller sends and simultaneously allows credentialed cross-origin requests, with no allow-list check anywhere. This middleware is not dead code: packages/backend/src/index.ts:33 does `app.use(corsMiddleware)` globally, ahead of `authMiddleware`/routes, and the Orion graph confirms the calls inside patchedCors (lines 6-22) are `reachable_from_entry=true` at `hop_distance=1`. A repo-wide grep for helmet/CSP/X-Frame-Options/csrf/allowlist-style constants in the backend returned nothing, corroborating that no compensating origin-allow-list or other control exists. This is a genuine CWE-942 misconfiguration. One nuance I verified that tempers severity vs. the analyst's framing: the app's auth is pure Bearer-JWT stored in browser localStorage and attached manually via the `Authorization` header (packages/frontend/utils/fetcher.ts, auth/utils.ts) \u2014 there is no cookie-based session anywhere in the backend (`ctx.cookies`/Set-Cookie grep returned 0 hits) \u2014 so a malicious third-party page cannot have the browser *automatically* attach a victim's credential the way the classic cookie+CORS attack requires; exploiting the authenticated endpoints via this hole alone would still need the attacker to already possess the bearer token (e.g. via XSS) rather than relying purely on ambient browser credentials. The misconfiguration itself, and its applicability to any unauthenticated/public endpoints plus any future cookie-based auth, is still real and should be fixed with a strict origin allow-list."
          },
