          "ruleId": "CWE-862",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "projects.delete('/:projectId') only checks that the CALLER's own org (ctx.state.orgId) has more than one project before deleting; it never verifies that the target :projectId actually belongs to ctx.state.orgId. A user in Org A with >1 project can delete any other org's project by supplying its id.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read the real source directly: in packages/backend/src/api/v1/projects/index.ts the DELETE \"/:projectId\" handler (lines 70-89) takes `projectId` from ctx.params (attacker-controlled route param) and `orgId` from ctx.state (the caller's own org, set from their JWT in authMiddleware). It runs `select count(*)::int from project where org_id = ${orgId}` \u2014 counting the CALLER's own org's projects \u2014 and if that count > 1, runs `delete from project where id = ${projectId}` with NO `org_id` predicate at all. The count check and the delete operate on disjoint row sets: the former never establishes that `projectId` belongs to `orgId`. I also checked the auth middleware (api/v1/auth/utils.ts:81-138): it only validates project ownership when `ctx.state.projectId` is set, and that field is populated from `ctx.request.query.projectId` (line 82), NOT from the route's `ctx.params.projectId`. Since this DELETE route supplies projectId via the URL path param, ctx.state.projectId stays undefined and the ownership check at utils.ts:117-126 is skipped entirely \u2014 there is no other gate anywhere in the request path that ties the target project to the caller's org. Route is mounted via v1.use(projects.routes()) under prefix /v1 (api/v1/index.ts) and is not in the authMiddleware publicRoutes allowlist (auth/utils.ts:62-78), so it requires only a valid bearer token for ANY org, not special privilege. Net effect: any authenticated user whose own org has >1 project can delete an arbitrary project belonging to a different org by supplying that project's id \u2014 a cross-tenant IDOR on a destructive action. (Additional aggravating note: project.id is also used as the project's public API key \u2014 see projects/index.ts:53-57 \u2014 so target project ids are often discoverable via public-facing artifacts, lowering the bar to exploit further.) Graph query showed reachable_from_entry=false/hop_distance=-1 for every call in this handler, but that's the documented static-analysis blind spot for calls inside arrow-function callbacks passed as router handler arguments (e.g. projects.delete(path, asyncFn) never gets linked) \u2014 source and mount-chain confirm real HTTP reachability, so I do not weigh the false reachable_from_entry flag against the finding."
