            "text": "versions.patch('/:id') (template_version) updates content/extra/testValues/isDraft for an arbitrary template_version id with no project ownership check at all -- unlike templates.patch('/:id') and templates.delete('/:id') in the sibling file which both filter by 'project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId}'. Any authenticated caller can overwrite any other project's prompt template content by id.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified directly against source (not the analyst's claim). packages/backend/src/api/v1/templateVersions.ts:57-77 defines versions.patch(\"/:id\"), which runs `update template_version set content=..., extra=..., test_values=..., is_draft=... where id = ${ctx.params.id} returning *` \u2014 the WHERE clause only filters on the caller-supplied `id`; `ctx.state.projectId` never appears in this handler. By contrast, the sibling file templates.ts filters every mutating route by project: templates.delete(\"/:id\") (line 79: `delete from template where project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and id = ...`) and templates.patch(\"/:id\") (line 95: `update template set ... where project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and id = ...`). I also checked the auth layer (api/v1/auth/utils.ts authMiddleware, applied globally in src/index.ts before v1 routes): it requires a valid JWT bearer token for any non-public path (template_versions PATCH is not in the publicRoutes list, only `/v1/template_versions/latest` GET is), and it only validates project ownership `if (ctx.state.projectId)` is present as a query param and belongs to the caller's org \u2014 it never ties the authorization check to the `:id` path parameter used by this route. So any authenticated user of any org can PATCH /v1/template_versions/<arbitrary-id> and overwrite another project's template_version row with attacker-controlled content/extra/testValues/isDraft, with the full updated row echoed back. This is a textbook IDOR / missing object-level authorization (CWE-862). Graph query of CpgCall nodes