            "text": "versions.get('/:id') (template_version) fetches the full template_version row by id BEFORE any ownership check, then separately queries 'template where project_id = ... and id = version.templateId' only to attach a (possibly undefined) 'template' field. The version's own content/extra is placed in ctx.body regardless of whether the ownership query matched, so a caller from a different project still receives the full template_version content (an authorization bypass via incomplete check), and if the ownership query returns nothing the code additionally throws on 'version.extra' uncamel on an undefined row only in the no-row case -- in the row-exists case content leaks unconditionally.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Read the live source at packages/backend/src/api/v1/templateVersions.ts:43-55. The handler `versions.get(\"/:id\", ...)` runs `select * from template_version where id = ${ctx.params.id}` with no project/org scoping whatsoever, then unconditionally does `ctx.body = { ...version, template }` where `template` comes from a *separate* query (`select * from template where project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId} and id = ${version.templateId}`) that is only used to attach an optional `template` field \u2014 its result (even if empty/undefined) never gates whether `version`'s own fields (content, extra, test_values, etc.) are returned. So the full template_version row is returned regardless of the caller's project.  I confirmed this route is actually mounted and reachable: it's registered via `v1.use(templateVersions.routes())` in packages/backend/src/api/v1/index.ts, and `authMiddleware` (packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts) only requires a valid JWT (any org/user) \u2014 `ctx.state.projectId` is taken from an optional query string param and, if present, is merely checked to belong to the caller's own org; it is never required to match the template_version being fetched, and this route doesn't even use it on the primary lookup. I also checked packages/db/init.sql:162-163: `template_version` uses `id serial primary key` \u2014 a plain sequential integer, not a UUID \u2014 so cross-tenant IDs are trivially enumerable (id=1,2,3,...), making the bypass practical, not merely theoretical. The CpgCall graph marks this entire file's calls (including the clearly-live `/latest` route) as `reachable_from_entry=false` and no EntryPoint node matches 'template' \u2014 this is the known static-graph gap for koa-router `.get()`/`.patch()` registrations, not evidence the route is dead; source-level wiring proves it is a live, authenticated API endpoint. The analyst's secondary remark (that `version.extra = unCamelObject(version.extra)` throws when no row matches the id) is also accurate for the case the *version* query misses \u2014 `version` would be undefined and assigning `.extra` on it throws \u2014 but that's a minor side detail; the core unconditional-leak claim is the confirmed finding."
