          "ruleId": "CWE-863",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "In authMiddleware (packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts), a bearer token on a 'public' route is first checked against validateUUID() before being trusted as ctx.state.projectId (lines 89-93), but immediately below it a second, redundant `if (isPublicRoute)` block (lines 95-102) re-extracts the same bearer token and sets ctx.state.projectId = bearerToken whenever it is merely `typeof bearerToken === 'string'` -- with no UUID/format validation at all. Any request to a public route (matched via the publicRoutes list, e.g. the regexes `/v1/datasets/.+`, `/v1/runs/.+/public`, `/v1/runs/.+/feedback`, or string routes like `/v1/runs/ingest`, `/v1/template_versions/latest`) whose Authorization header is a non-UUID string falls through the first guard (since `!bearerToken` is false and `validateUUID(bearerToken)` is false) and lands in the second block, which accepts the raw token as the project scope unchecked. This looks like a validation fix (the UUID check) that was added in one branch while the old, unguarded code path was left intact directly beneath it -- a classic half-reverted protection. Downstream handlers (e.g. packages/backend/src/api/v1/templates.ts, templateVersions.ts) use ctx.state.projectId directly as the tenant-scoping key in SQL queries, so an attacker who can reach any publicRoutes-matched path can set projectId to an arbitrary string and potentially access/mutate data belonging to another project/org.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived independently from the real source (not the analyst's say-so). Read packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/utils.ts directly: lines 88-106 contain exactly the two blocks described -- `if (isPublicRoute && (!bearerToken || validateUUID(bearerToken)))` (90-96), which only sets ctx.state.projectId = bearerToken when validateUUID() passes (line 91-92), followed immediately by a second `if (isPublicRoute)` (98-106) that re-reads the same header and sets ctx.state.projectId = bearerToken whenever `typeof bearerToken === \"string\"`, with zero format check. Traced the control flow by hand: when isPublicRoute=true and bearerToken is a non-empty, non-UUID string, the first condition (90) evaluates isPublicRoute(true) && (false || false) = false, so the block is skipped; isPublicRoute is still true, so the second block (98) unconditionally executes and assigns the raw attacker string to ctx.state.projectId. Confirmed validateUUID in packages/backend/src/utils/misc.ts:20-24 is a real zod `.uuid()` format check. Confirmed reachability via the code graph (scan_id 39391b6ee394d4f8ba76da9837645885da014f16): CpgMethod authMiddleware has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=0; the specific sink call at line 102 (`ctx.state.projectId = bearerToken`, uid 98f80f4a4fa73d35f0ac105c66210427ae129fab) has reachable_from_entry=true, hop_distance=1 -- this is live, reachable code, not dead code. Confirmed downstream sinks in templates.ts (lines 15, 71, 79, 95) and templateVersions.ts (line 51) use ctx.state.projectId directly as `project_id = ${ctx.state.projectId}` in parameterized SQL, and that `/v1/template_versions/latest` is itself in the publicRoutes list, so this path is reachable from a public, unauthenticated route. However, I also checked packages/db/init.sql and every project_id column referenced by these sinks (project.id, template.project_id, dataset.project_id, run.project_id, external_user.project_id, log.project_id) is declared `uuid` type. Because the `postgres` driver (packages/backend/src/utils/db.ts) binds these as inferred/untyped parameters against a uuid column, a non-UUID garbage string reaching these queries would fail Postgres's UUID parse at bind time (invalid input syntax for type uuid) rather than successfully matching or mutating another tenant's row -- so the specific \"attacker sets projectId to an arbitrary string and reads/writes another org's data\" narrative is not substantiated by any sink I could find; the demonstrated effect of this bug is an unvalidated value reaching a security-relevant SQL parameter and most likely causing a query error for malformed tokens, not a proven cross-tenant data breach. The underlying flaw (inconsistent/redundant validation letting a security-relevant state variable bypass its intended format check) is real and reachable, so I confirm the defect, but at reduced severity versus the analyst's implied HIGH/CRITICAL, since no sink I inspected demonstrates actual unauthorized data exposure from this specific bypass (a well-formed UUID bypasses the format check too, but that is the pre-existing, apparently intentional \"project id acts as public key\" design of these public routes, not something newly introduced by the redundant block)."
