          "ruleId": "CWE-287",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Email-ownership verification can be fully disabled via the `SKIP_EMAIL_VERIFY` environment flag: in the `/auth/signup` handler (packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/index.ts, line 62) a newly-created account's `verified` field is set from `process.env.SKIP_EMAIL_VERIFY ? true : false`, and `sendVerifyEmail` (packages/backend/src/utils/emails.ts, line 9-10) early-returns without sending any verification email whenever the same flag is set: `if (process.env.SKIP_EMAIL_VERIFY) { return }`. If this flag is left set (e.g. carried over from a dev/test environment into production), every new signup is marked verified without ever proving control of the email address, undermining the email-verification control. Confidence is lower because the only enforcement of `verified` found in this sweep is a frontend UI nag banner (packages/frontend/components/Layout/Navbar.tsx), not a hard backend authorization gate, so the blast radius of this bypass is unclear without deeper access-control analysis.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Re-derived directly from source (not from the analyst's claim). In packages/backend/src/api/v1/auth/index.ts, `auth.post(\"/signup\", ...)` (line 14) is mounted live and unauthenticated via `app.use(auth.routes())` in packages/backend/src/index.ts:42 \u2014 this is a real, reachable public endpoint, not a hypothetical. Inside the \"signup\" branch, the new account object built at lines 56-63 sets `verified: process.env.SKIP_EMAIL_VERIFY ? true : false` (line 62) \u2014 a self-asserted \"verified\" flag with zero proof of email ownership whenever the env var is truthy. `sendVerifyEmail` in packages/backend/src/utils/emails.ts lines 9-12 independently early-returns and never sends the confirmation email under the same condition, so the bypass is total (no email sent, account marked verified). I confirmed via grep across the whole backend that `verified` is never used as an access-control gate anywhere outside the signup/verify-email code itself (packages/backend/src/api/v1/users.ts only reads it back for display and for the already-verified short-circuit in `/users/verify-email`; packages/backend/src/api/v1/orgs.ts only selects the column) and the only consumer outside the backend is the frontend nag banner (Navbar.tsx:125) \u2014 matching the analyst's \"unclear blast radius\" caveat. I found two things the analyst did not: (1) .env.example ships `SKIP_EMAIL_VERIFY=true` by default with the comment \"Set to false to enable email verification\" \u2014 but since process.env values are always strings and any non-empty string is truthy in JS, setting it to the literal string \"false\" does NOT disable the bypass (only unsetting/deleting the var does) \u2014 this is a real footgun that makes the misconfiguration more likely to survive into production than the comment implies; and (2) the \"join\" signup branch (line 124) sets `verified: true` unconditionally regardless of the flag, confirming `verified` was never treated as a hard security boundary in this codebase even by its own authors. Graph query confirmed both cited CpgCall nodes exist with the exact code/line (uid 32f9690e..., line 62; uid 24c9e4d1..., line 9) but `reachable_from_entry=false` for both \u2014 I attribute this to the known graph gap for calls nested inside arrow-function arguments passed to router methods (e.g. `auth.post(\"/signup\", async (ctx) => {...})`), since no EntryPoint/ENTERS_AT edge exists for this file at all in the graph, yet the source and the app bootstrap unambiguously prove it is a live mounted route. I down-weight severity relative to a hard auth-bypass because no backend code was found that denies unverified accounts any capability \u2014 the practical impact is limited to defeating the \"prove you own this email\" trust signal (enabling email squatting/impersonation and disabling the verification email) rather than a direct privilege escalation."
