            "text": "In the LLM-run 'checks' evaluation engine, the 'regex' check type builds a RegExp directly from a fully attacker-supplied pattern string (params.regex) with no validation, size limit, or safe-regex guard, then runs it with .test()/.match() against run message content inside the reachable EntryPoint method `evaluator` (packages/backend/src/checks/index.ts). Because the attacker controls the ENTIRE pattern (not just a fixed literal), they can submit a catastrophic-backtracking pattern (e.g. `(a+)+$`) as a saved check/radar filter and trigger a Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) that blocks the Node.js event loop whenever that check runs against any matching run text -- and since these checks can be persisted as recurring 'Radar' jobs (packages/backend/src/jobs/radar.ts calls runRadarChecksOnRun -> runChecksOnRun -> evaluator), the DoS can repeat automatically against the whole dataset.\n\nVerifier (CONFIRM): Verified end-to-end in source (not graph alone). The 'regex' CHECK_RUNNERS entry builds `new RegExp(regex)` from `params.regex` with zero validation/size-limit/safe-regex guard, then runs `.test()`/`.match()` on it (checks/index.ts:96-113). `params` reaches here via checks/runChecks.ts:83-98 (`checkRun` -> `runner.evaluator!(run, params)`), called from `runChecksOnRun`. Two independent attacker-controlled persistence paths feed this: (1) `POST /v1/radars` (api/v1/radars.ts:217-241) stores an unvalidated `checks` array from the request body as `radar.checks`; the always-running background loop `runRadarJob`/`radarJob` (jobs/radar.ts:46-105, started from src/radar.ts) repeatedly calls `runRadarChecksOnRun` -> `runChecksOnRun` against batches of up to 300 runs, so a saved catastrophic-backtracking pattern (e.g. `(a+)+$`) re-triggers automatically and repeatedly. (2) `POST /v1/checklists` (api/v1/checklists.ts:33-52) stores an unvalidated `data` (FilterLogic) blob; `POST /v1/evaluations/run` (api/v1/evaluations/index.ts:102-159) loads that data and calls `runChecksOnRun` SYNCHRONOUSLY inside the main API request handler, making this directly, synchronously reachable from an authenticated HTTP request, not just a side job. A repo-wide grep confirms the only dynamic, fully-attacker-supplied `new RegExp()` call in the backend is this one; all others are fixed literals, and no safe-regex/timeout wrapper exists anywhere. The analyst's graph-derived claim that these calls belong to a CpgMethod 'evaluator' at line 471 is itself incorrect -- line 471 is the unrelated 'toxicity' evaluator; the CPG appears to collapse multiple same-named arrow/async 'evaluator' functions into one mislabeled symbol. This graph artifact doesn't undermine the finding since I verified the real code and full data-flow directly from source."
